<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is allowed, and this is a deliberate trade rather than an
  oversight.

  Reasonix's own surfaces speak plain HTTP: a kernel serves its page on
  `127.0.0.1:<port>`, and the Studio on a computer serves the same page on its
  LAN address so a phone can pair with it. Both are `http://`. Android denies
  cleartext by default from API 28, so without this the WebView could load
  neither — including the paired computer this app exists to reach.

  The alternative, naming hosts, cannot express the thing that needs it: the
  address of the computer to pair with is whatever its router handed out
  (`192.168.x.x`), chosen at scan time, and `network-security-config` matches
  literals and domains, not subnets. So the exception is global.

  What that costs is bounded by what this app can reach. Its own kernel listens
  on loopback; a paired computer is on the same network; a remote server is
  reached over SSH, which is encrypted whatever the tunnelled protocol is. The
  page it loads is the same build this repository ships, and the credential it
  trades a pairing code for is an HttpOnly cookie scoped to that origin.

  Still HTTPS everywhere it is offered: nothing here downgrades a request, and
  `trust-anchors` keeps the system roots for every TLS connection that is made.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
